Start();
include(unitTests);
include(utilities);
search reset stack Grokking;
include("IncantForms/WorkingOn/parser");
define
    llSay code={
        v := tell(argument);
        m := *v["matched"]; c := *v["consumed"];
        cerr "LL-DRIVE " argument " matched= " m " consumed= " c:;
        rp = 0;
        if *v["reply"];     rp = 1;
        if rp == 1;     labelSpans(*v["reply"]);
        if rp == 0;     cerr "SPAN (no reply)":;
        };
    ;
register(LeafLab);
define
    LlOUT isRule lp-="(" wd=[a-z]+ rp-=")";
    ;
search GroupFields UnitTests Utilities LeafLab list;
cerr "--- OLD ROAD":;
llSay("LlOUT (abc)");
parser(LlOUT);
cerr "--- NEW ROAD":;
llSay("LlOUT (abc)");
llSay("LlOUT (xyzw)");
cerr "LEAFLABEL SENTINEL":;
stop();

leafLabelT -- A NEW-ROAD LEAF'S LABEL, READ BACK BY VALUE (SEQ 302 R1; 1.2f leans on this row).

LlOUT's middle term `wd=[a-z]+` is a labelled SET leaf: on the new road it is parseSet, which since 1.2c keeps its label
in a local and hands it to exitFromParse, where it attaches under LlOUT's label. labelSpans prints the reply tree with
each node's tag and the span it covers, so the row reads the leaf's TAG (wd) and its TEXT (abc, then xyzw) by value.
The old road runs first (parser() is one-way) as the comparison: the same tree, the same span.

Before this row the leaf-label handoff's only witness was leafClassT, which crashed when the label went missing. H7
(the leaves hand the exit no label), this fixture alone under an alarm: the wd node is absent from the new-road tree
-- a wrong VALUE, not a crash.

labelSpans is a printer beside labelTree (measure.twk): labelTree prints tags only, and a kant subscript could not reach
the child label of tell's reply (`*lt["wd"]` and `*lt[1]` both read nothing; measured 2026-10-05).
